CUDA Streams cuDNN Image Processor - installation and execution
===============================================================

Requirements
------------

- NVIDIA GPU and a current NVIDIA driver.
- CUDA Toolkit 12.x or a version compatible with the selected cuDNN release.
- NVIDIA cuDNN SDK for Windows. It is a separate installation from CUDA Toolkit.
- CMake 3.24 or later.
- Visual Studio 2022 with Desktop development with C++ (Windows) or a supported
  C++ compiler (Linux).

cuDNN setup
-----------

Install cuDNN from NVIDIA. The CMake configuration needs its root directory -
the folder containing `include/cudnn.h` and `lib/x64/cudnn.lib` on Windows.

Windows (Git Bash or PowerShell)
-------------------------------

From the project root, replace the example path with your actual cuDNN folder:

  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCUDNN_ROOT="C:/path/to/cudnn"
  cmake --build build --config Release
  bash run.sh

Linux
-----

If cuDNN is installed system-wide, CMake often finds it automatically:

  cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  cmake --build build --config Release
  ./run.sh

Otherwise, set CUDNN_ROOT to the cuDNN installation root as in the Windows
example.

For a cuDNN 9 Windows installation with multiple CUDA variants, CMake selects
the first compatible variant automatically. To choose one explicitly, add
`-DCUDNN_CUDA_VARIANT=12.9` to the configure command.

Quick smoke test
----------------

  bash run.sh --images 16 --width 256 --height 256 --streams 2

All runs write `input_0000.pgm` and `cudnn_convolution_result_0000.pgm` to
`data/results/`.
